  },
  "actual_proposed_EC": {
    "when": "When ARIZONA is allowable and an asset matches an Arizona occupied-homestead, household-goods, wearing-apparel, musical-instrument, engagement-or-wedding-ring, motor-vehicle, prescribed-aid, firearm, or domestic-animal category.",
    "procedure": "Use the substantive asset exemption: § 33-1101(A) for a qualifying occupied homestead; § 33-1123 for household furniture, furnishings, goods, electronics, or appliances not otherwise specifically prescribed; and § 33-1125(1) for actual wearing apparel, (2) for a musical instrument used by the debtor or family, (4) only for engagement or wedding rings, (8) for a motor vehicle, (9) for a professionally prescribed prosthesis or stated physician-authorized mobility or respiratory aid, (10) for firearms, and (11) for domestic animals or household pets. A stated value above the statutory ceiling still permits the EC citation.",
    "avoid_when": "Do not cite § 33-1101(B) or § 33-1121 merely because the debtors are married. Do not treat jewelry as wearing apparel under § 33-1125(1), and do not place jewelry under § 33-1123. A pendant, necklace, bracelet, earrings, brooch, anniversary ring, or other jewelry does not qualify under § 33-1125(4) without engagement-or-wedding-ring facts. Do not add § 33-1123 to apparel, musical instruments, prescribed aids, firearms, motor vehicles, or pets specifically prescribed by another listed provision.",
    "operators": [],
    "jurisdiction": "ARIZONA"
  },
  "change_scope": "AZ EC category/exclusion revision: musical instruments, explicit jewelry/household distinctions and classification independent of the value ceiling. Actual replay determines usefulness; no assertion that every statutory category must be enumerated in a skill.",
